{
  "id": "future-conditional",
  "title": "Future, conditional and past conditional",
  "priority": 2,
  "order": 3,
  "explanation": "Three ways to talk about \"not now\" (sheet):\n- **imparfait** for the past as background: je pensais (I was thinking / used to think), je dormais, il y avait\n- **futur simple** for a plain future: je penserai (I will think), je dormirai, il y aura\n- **futur proche**, aller + infinitive, for what is about to happen or is planned: je vais y penser, je vais dormir\n\nIn speech the futur proche does most of the work. Faire in all three: je faisais / je ferai / je vais faire. Note the irregular futur simple stems: je ferai, j'irai, je serai, j'aurai, je pourrai, je devrai, je saurai.\n\n**Conditional** softens: je voudrais (I'd like), ce serait mieux (that would be better), je devrais (I should), tu croirais ça ? (would you believe that?). It is the futur simple stem plus imparfait endings: je ferais, je serais, je devrais.\n\n**Past conditional** for what would have happened: j'aurais su (I would have known), ça aurait dû être (it should have been). It is the conditional of avoir or être plus the participle. With a si-clause: *si tu ne m'avais pas dit que tu étais au lit, je n'aurais pas su* (if you hadn't told me, I wouldn't have known). The si-clause takes the pluperfect (avais dit), never the conditional.",
